1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,851
Welcome to Lesson Two of Renaissance Field Lite's Introduction to

2
00:00:03,851 --> 00:00:07,352
Quantum Computing. In Lesson One, we compared certain zero, certain

3
00:00:07,352 --> 00:00:10,852
one, and equal superposition. Now we replace those fixed anchor

4
00:00:10,852 --> 00:00:15,053
states with a controllable R Y rotation and watch the probability of

5
00:00:15,053 --> 00:00:17,504
measuring one change smoothly with the angle.

6
00:00:17,884 --> 00:00:20,306
Lesson One gave us three anchor points.

7
00:00:20,306 --> 00:00:23,767
Zero degrees corresponds to certain zero, ninety degrees gives the

8
00:00:23,767 --> 00:00:26,881
familiar near fifty-fifty measurement pattern, and one hundred

9
00:00:26,881 --> 00:00:28,958
eighty degrees corresponds to certain one.

10
00:00:28,958 --> 00:00:33,110
The new question is what happens at the angles between those points.

11
00:00:33,490 --> 00:00:36,826
The objective is to calculate the expected probability for each

12
00:00:36,826 --> 00:00:40,828
angle, run seven one-qubit circuits for one thousand shots each, and

13
00:00:40,828 --> 00:00:43,497
compare the measured frequencies with the theoretical curve.

14
00:00:43,497 --> 00:00:47,499
We hold the simulator, seed, and shot count constant so the rotation

15
00:00:47,499 --> 00:00:49,500
angle is the only controlled change.

16
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:55,461
The R Y gate rotates the qubit state around the Y axis by an angle

17
00:00:55,461 --> 00:00:59,181
theta. At theta equals zero, the state remains ket zero.

18
00:00:59,181 --> 00:01:02,529
As theta increases, more measurement probability moves toward one.

19
00:01:02,529 --> 00:01:06,994
At theta equals pi, or one hundred eighty degrees, the state reaches

20
00:01:06,994 --> 00:01:07,738
ket one.

21
00:01:08,118 --> 00:01:11,729
For this circuit, the expected probability of measuring one is sine

22
00:01:11,729 --> 00:01:15,997
squared of theta over two. We do not need a full derivation yet.

23
00:01:15,997 --> 00:01:19,937
The practical point is that the formula maps each rotation angle to

24
00:01:19,937 --> 00:01:21,578
a specific expected measurement frequency.

25
00:01:21,958 --> 00:01:25,417
Before running the code, we can predict three checkpoints.

26
00:01:25,417 --> 00:01:28,876
At zero degrees, the probability of one is zero.

27
00:01:28,876 --> 00:01:31,566
At ninety degrees, it is one half.

28
00:01:31,566 --> 00:01:34,640
At one hundred eighty degrees, it is one.

29
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:38,483
These predictions let us verify the sweep before examining the

30
00:01:38,483 --> 00:01:39,252
intermediate angles.

31
00:01:39,632 --> 00:01:43,859
The source sets shots to one thousand, fixes the simulator seed at

32
00:01:43,859 --> 00:01:47,733
sixty-seven, and defines seven angles from zero through pi in

33
00:01:47,733 --> 00:01:50,904
thirty-degree steps. Using one shared list prevents accidental

34
00:01:50,904 --> 00:01:54,074
changes between the theoretical calculation and the circuits that

35
00:01:54,074 --> 00:01:55,131
are actually executed.

36
00:01:55,511 --> 00:01:59,929
The circuit builder creates one qubit, applies R Y with the supplied

37
00:01:59,929 --> 00:02:01,770
angle, and measures the result.

38
00:02:01,770 --> 00:02:03,979
No other gate changes between experiments.

39
00:02:03,979 --> 00:02:08,029
This makes the lesson a clean parameter sweep rather than seven

40
00:02:08,029 --> 00:02:09,133
unrelated circuit examples.

41
00:02:09,513 --> 00:02:13,305
The main function builds all seven circuits, executes them together

42
00:02:13,305 --> 00:02:17,096
with the Statevector Sampler, and then processes the results in

43
00:02:17,096 --> 00:02:20,888
matching order. For each angle, observed probability is the number

44
00:02:20,888 --> 00:02:24,680
of ones divided by one thousand, while expected probability comes

45
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:26,576
from the sine-squared formula.

46
00:02:26,956 --> 00:02:31,821
Now we execute the preserved Lesson Two Python file in the local

47
00:02:31,821 --> 00:02:35,471
Qiskit environment. The output table places the angle, theoretical

48
00:02:35,471 --> 00:02:39,931
probability, measured probability, and raw counts on the same row so

49
00:02:39,931 --> 00:02:42,363
every prediction can be checked directly.

50
00:02:42,743 --> 00:02:46,477
At zero degrees, expected probability of one is zero, observed

51
00:02:46,477 --> 00:02:50,211
probability is zero, and all one thousand measurements are zero.

52
00:02:50,211 --> 00:02:54,317
This is the unchanged starting state and the first exact endpoint

53
00:02:54,317 --> 00:02:54,691
check.

54
00:02:55,071 --> 00:02:59,121
At thirty degrees, the expected probability of one is about six

55
00:02:59,121 --> 00:03:02,804
point seven percent. The simulation returns seventy ones, or seven

56
00:03:02,804 --> 00:03:06,854
percent. The observed value differs by only three tenths of one

57
00:03:06,854 --> 00:03:07,591
percentage point.

58
00:03:07,971 --> 00:03:11,931
At sixty degrees, the expected probability is twenty-five percent.

59
00:03:11,931 --> 00:03:16,684
The run produces two hundred forty-eight ones, or twenty-four point

60
00:03:16,684 --> 00:03:20,248
eight percent. Again, finite sampling shifts the exact count

61
00:03:20,248 --> 00:03:22,625
slightly while preserving the predicted probability.

62
00:03:23,005 --> 00:03:25,836
At ninety degrees, the formula predicts one half.

63
00:03:25,836 --> 00:03:29,728
The run produces four hundred ninety-three ones and five hundred

64
00:03:29,728 --> 00:03:34,328
seven zeros. This is the same seeded near-even split seen in Lesson

65
00:03:34,328 --> 00:03:37,867
One, now located as the midpoint of a continuous rotation.

66
00:03:38,247 --> 00:03:42,091
At one hundred twenty degrees, expected probability is seventy-five

67
00:03:42,091 --> 00:03:46,319
percent. The run produces seven hundred fifty ones and two hundred

68
00:03:46,319 --> 00:03:50,163
fifty zeros, matching the theoretical value exactly in this seeded

69
00:03:50,163 --> 00:03:50,547
sample.

70
00:03:50,927 --> 00:03:53,999
At one hundred fifty degrees, expected probability is about

71
00:03:53,999 --> 00:03:55,706
ninety-three point three percent.

72
00:03:55,706 --> 00:03:59,461
The run produces nine hundred thirty-nine ones, or ninety-three

73
00:03:59,461 --> 00:04:03,898
point nine percent. The state is close to certain one but has not

74
00:04:03,898 --> 00:04:05,264
yet reached the endpoint.

75
00:04:05,644 --> 00:04:09,009
At one hundred eighty degrees, expected and observed probability

76
00:04:09,009 --> 00:04:12,374
both equal one. All one thousand measurements are one.

77
00:04:12,374 --> 00:04:16,860
The sweep has moved continuously from the original zero state to the

78
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:18,356
opposite computational basis state.

79
00:04:18,736 --> 00:04:23,103
The chart shows the theoretical sine-squared curve and the seven

80
00:04:23,103 --> 00:04:24,294
measured frequencies together.

81
00:04:24,294 --> 00:04:29,059
The observed points track the expected values from zero to one, with

82
00:04:29,059 --> 00:04:32,632
only small finite-sample differences at the non-deterministic

83
00:04:32,632 --> 00:04:33,029
angles.

84
00:04:33,409 --> 00:04:35,203
The verification has three parts.

85
00:04:35,203 --> 00:04:38,792
Both endpoints are exact, the ninety-degree midpoint is near

86
00:04:38,792 --> 00:04:42,022
fifty-fifty, and each intermediate measurement frequency stays close

87
00:04:42,022 --> 00:04:44,534
to the value calculated from the formula.

88
00:04:44,534 --> 00:04:48,122
Together, these checks confirm that the code and the probability

89
00:04:48,122 --> 00:04:48,840
model agree.

90
00:04:49,220 --> 00:04:52,997
A probability of twenty-five percent does not guarantee exactly two

91
00:04:52,997 --> 00:04:56,088
hundred fifty ones in every one-thousand-shot run.

92
00:04:56,088 --> 00:04:58,491
The measured count is a finite sample.

93
00:04:58,491 --> 00:05:02,269
Changing the seed can change the exact numbers, while repeated runs

94
00:05:02,269 --> 00:05:04,672
still cluster around the same theoretical curve.

95
00:05:05,052 --> 00:05:07,415
This is an ideal local simulation.

96
00:05:07,415 --> 00:05:11,353
A physical quantum processor can add gate errors, readout errors,

97
00:05:11,353 --> 00:05:13,323
calibration drift, and device constraints.

98
00:05:13,323 --> 00:05:18,048
The simulator is appropriate here because the goal is to isolate how

99
00:05:18,048 --> 00:05:21,593
the R Y angle controls the intended measurement probability.

100
00:05:21,973 --> 00:05:25,759
The result is reproducible because the source is preserved, the seed

101
00:05:25,759 --> 00:05:29,546
is fixed at sixty-seven, every angle uses exactly one thousand

102
00:05:29,546 --> 00:05:32,988
shots, and the raw counts are recorded beside the theoretical

103
00:05:32,988 --> 00:05:36,774
values. Another user can rerun the same file and inspect the

104
00:05:36,774 --> 00:05:37,463
complete comparison.

105
00:05:37,843 --> 00:05:41,792
Lesson Two shows how one gate controls a single-qubit probability

106
00:05:41,792 --> 00:05:45,022
distribution. In Lesson Three, we add a second qubit.

107
00:05:45,022 --> 00:05:48,971
A Hadamard gate creates the probability split, and a controlled X

108
00:05:48,971 --> 00:05:52,202
gate turns that split into correlated two-qubit outcomes.

109
00:05:52,582 --> 00:05:56,137
That completes Lesson Two of Renaissance Field Lite's Introduction

110
00:05:56,137 --> 00:06:00,047
to Quantum Computing. We used real code, measured output, and a

111
00:06:00,047 --> 00:06:03,957
theoretical curve to verify probability control with the R Y gate.

112
00:06:03,957 --> 00:06:06,801
The runnable Lesson Two code is linked below.

113
00:06:06,801 --> 00:06:10,356
Follow Renaissance Field Lite and Codex 67 for the full

114
00:06:10,356 --> 00:06:11,422
twelve-lesson series.
